class:: Scangen
summary:: Scanned synthesis on a dynamic mass-spring lattice
related:: Classes/Oscnet, Classes/Lifestoch, Classes/Ecostoch
categories:: UGens>Generators>Chaotic

description::

Scanned synthesis after Verplank/Mathews. A ring of code::size:: masses connected by
springs evolves as a slow dynamical system (its speed set by code::rate::, decoupled from
pitch), while a scan pointer reads the displacement profile around the ring at audio rate
as a continuously-morphing wavetable. A trickle of code::excite:: energy keeps the lattice
alive; code::damping:: and code::center:: bleed and recentre it; code::nonlin:: adds a
cubic spring stiffness so large excursions distort and the timbre evolves unpredictably.

This is a persistent dynamical instrument: it holds state and drifts. code::size:: is fixed
at build time.

classmethods::

method:: ar
argument:: freq
Scan frequency (Hz) — the pitch. Default 110.
argument:: size
Number of masses in the ring (fixed). Default 64.
argument:: rate
Lattice evolution speed, 0–0.5 (decoupled from pitch). Default 0.05.
argument:: tension
Spring stiffness between neighbours, 0–0.49. Default 0.2.
argument:: damping
Velocity damping, 0–1. Default 0.005.
argument:: center
Restoring force toward rest, 0–1. Default 0.001.
argument:: nonlin
Cubic (nonlinear) spring stiffness. Default 0.
argument:: excite
Continuous random excitation energy. Default 0.01.
argument:: mul
argument:: add

examples::
code::
// a slowly morphing wavetable tone
{ Scangen.ar(110, 64, 0.05, 0.2, 0.01) * 0.3 ! 2 }.play;

// evolution speed: from near-static wavetable to churning texture
{ Scangen.ar(110, 64, MouseX.kr(0.005, 0.4), 0.2, 0.02) * 0.3 ! 2 }.play;

// tension sweeps the lattice's resonant character
{ Scangen.ar(80, 96, 0.06, MouseX.kr(0.01, 0.48), 0.01) * 0.3 ! 2 }.play;

// nonlinear springs: large swings distort and destabilise the shape
{ Scangen.ar(90, 64, 0.08, 0.3, 0.005, 0.001, MouseX.kr(0, 4), 0.01) * 0.25 ! 2 }.play;
::
